1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 Na5 9. Bc2 c5 10. d3 ({The most frequently played variation of the Ruy Lopez, 10 d3 is an inactive kind of move. The more preferable} 10. d4 {would hold the centre.}) 10... O-O 11. Nbd2 Kh8 {This and the following two moves are of doubtful value. Black's defensive strength, how­ever, is deceptive, and any sudden attack against his King is liable to fail.} 12. Nf1 Ng8 13. Ng3 g6 14. d4 Nc6 15. dxc5 dxc5 16. Qe2 Be6 17. b3 Rc8 18. Rd1 Qa5 19. Bb2 c4 20. Nf5 {An exceptionally fine move that should have given White the ad­vantage. If 20...gxf5 21 gxf5, recovering the piece with the better game. After Black’s reply White should have played 21 Nd6 and won a pawn.} Bf6 21. Ne3 ({Stockfish 17 prefers the move played to Yates's} 21. Nd6) 21... Nce7 22. Nd5 ({Now} 22. a4 {would have developed the cramped queenside pieces. If} cxb3 23. axb5 Qxb5 24. Bd3 {and the a1-rook is strongly posted on an open file.}) 22... Nxd5 23. exd5 Bg4 24. h3 Bxf3 25. Qxf3 Qb6 26. b4 $2 {Making things safe on the queenside, but with the b2-bishop behind the blocked pawns it is a positional blunder, especi­ally as Black will shortly have time after Bg7 of advancing ...f7-f5, the original intention of ...Kh8 and ...Ng8.} Bg7 27. a4 f5 28. axb5 axb5 29. d6 Rfd8 30. Qd5 {An indifferent waste of time, the isolated pawn is bound to fall. After 30...Nf6 31 Qxe5 would be risky.} Nf6 31. Qd2 Rc6 32. d7 Rc7 33. Ra8 Rcxd7 34. Rxd8+ Qxd8 35. Qe2 Rxd1+ 36. Bxd1 Qd3 37. Qxd3 cxd3 38. c4 bxc4 39. Bxe5 Nd5 40. Bd6 c3 {Sources: BCM, April 1911, p173; The City of London Chess Club Championship by Roger Leslie Paige} 0-1

